# ============================================================================
# GitHub Repository Secrets Configuration
# ============================================================================
#
# This file documents the secrets you need to configure in your GitHub repository
# to use the reusable CI/CD workflows from the cicd_github repository.
#
# HOW TO ADD SECRETS TO YOUR REPOSITORY:
# 1. Go to your repository on GitHub
# 2. Navigate to: Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions
# 3. Click "New repository secret"
# 4. Add each secret listed below
#
# ============================================================================

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RELEASE_TOKEN (REQUIRED for CD workflow)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A Personal Access Token (PAT) with sufficient permissions to:
# - Push to protected main branch
# - Create releases and upload assets
# - Access the private cicd_github repository (if private)
#
# HOW TO CREATE:
# 1. Go to: https://github.com/settings/tokens?type=beta
# 2. Click "Generate new token" (Fine-grained tokens recommended)
# 3. Configure:
#    - Token name: RELEASE_TOKEN_YOUR_REPO_NAME
#    - Expiration: 90 days (set a calendar reminder to rotate)
#    - Repository access:
#      * "Only select repositories"
#      * Select YOUR repository (the one using workflows)
#      * Select cicd_github repository (to access workflows if private)
#    - Permissions:
#      * Contents: Read and write (push commits, tags, releases)
#      * Workflows: Read (access private workflow files)
#      * Metadata: Read-only (automatically selected)
# 4. Click "Generate token" and COPY IT IMMEDIATELY
# 5. Add to your repository as secret named: RELEASE_TOKEN
#
# SECURITY NOTE:
# - Never commit this token to git
# - Rotate every 90 days
# - Use fine-grained tokens (not classic PATs)
# - Limit to only required repositories
#
RELEASE_TOKEN=ghp_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx


# ============================================================================
# OPTIONAL: Alternative Secret Names
# ============================================================================
# If you have existing workflows using different secret names, you can:
# 1. Add the same PAT with multiple names, OR
# 2. Update your caller workflow to use: RELEASE_TOKEN
#
# Legacy secret names found in some projects:
# - SEM_RELEASE (used in older document_agent/document_search)
#
# We recommend standardizing on RELEASE_TOKEN for consistency.
# ============================================================================


# ============================================================================
# PyPI Tokens (Future Enhancement - Not Currently Used)
# ============================================================================
# If you want to publish to PyPI in the future, add:
#
# PYPI_TOKEN=pypi-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#
# HOW TO CREATE:
# 1. Go to: https://pypi.org/manage/account/token/
# 2. Create API token with scope: "Entire account" or specific project
# 3. Copy the token (starts with "pypi-")
# 4. Add to repository secrets as: PYPI_TOKEN
#
# Then update your reusable-cd.yml or create a PyPI-specific workflow.
# ============================================================================


# ============================================================================
# Workflow Access Token Alternative (For Organizations)
# ============================================================================
# If you're using GitHub Organizations, you might use a GitHub App instead:
#
# WORKFLOW_APP_ID=123456
# WORKFLOW_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----...
#
# This provides better security and audit controls for organizations.
# See: https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps
# ============================================================================
